Rule is strongest; policy, guidelines, regulations, and selected non-English
rule words also fire. Selected guidance, constraint, and programming terms
are weaker. Rule-like spelling fragments fire even in unrelated words.

Local phrases matter: 'rule(s) of' retains a signal on 'of', whereas 'the
following' and 'as follows' weaken the signal on their last word. Numeric
levels are rounded approximations from training examples. Unlisted tokens
get zero, leaving weak background responses unexplained. This does not test
whether a sentence actually expresses a rule.
